{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"DOI:10.1242/dev.175893","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"31253636","source":"MED","pmid":"31253636","doi":"10.1242/dev.175893","title":"Adaptive correction of craniofacial defects in pre-metamorphic <i>Xenopus laevis</i> tadpoles involves thyroid hormone-independent tissue remodeling.","authorString":"Pinet K, Deolankar M, Leung B, McLaughlin KA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Pinet K","lastName":"Pinet","firstName":"Kaylinnette","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, Allen Discovery Center, Tufts University, 200 Boston Ave., Suite 4700, Medford, MA 02155, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Deolankar M","lastName":"Deolankar","firstName":"Manas","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, Allen Discovery Center, Tufts University, 200 Boston Ave., Suite 4700, Medford, MA 02155, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Leung B","lastName":"Leung","firstName":"Brian","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, Allen Discovery Center, Tufts University, 200 Boston Ave., Suite 4700, Medford, MA 02155, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"McLaughlin KA","lastName":"McLaughlin","firstName":"Kelly A","initials":"KA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2541-2189"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, Allen Discovery Center, Tufts University, 200 Boston Ave., Suite 4700, Medford, MA 02155, USA kelly.mclaughlin@tufts.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2541-2189"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"14","volume":"146","journalIssueId":2839199,"dateOfPublication":"2019 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2019,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Development","title":"Development (Cambridge, England)","essn":"1477-9129","issn":"0950-1991","nlmid":"8701744","isoabbreviation":"Development"},"printPublicationDate":"2019-07-01"},"pubYear":"2019","pageInfo":"dev175893","abstractText":"Although it is well established that some organisms can regenerate lost structures, the ability to remodel existing malformed structures has been less well studied. Therefore, in this study we examined the ability of pre-metamorphic <i>Xenopus laevis</i> tadpoles to self-correct malformed craniofacial tissues. We found that tadpoles can adaptively improve and normalize abnormal craniofacial morphology caused by numerous developmental perturbations. We then investigated the tissue-level and molecular mechanisms that mediate the self-correction of craniofacial defects in pre-metamorphic <i>X. laevis</i> tadpoles. Our studies revealed that this adaptive response involves morphological changes and the remodeling of cartilage tissue, prior to metamorphosis. RT-qPCR and RNA-seq analysis of gene expression suggests a thyroid hormone-independent endocrine signaling pathway as the potential mechanism responsible for triggering the adaptive and corrective remodeling response in these larvae that involves <i>mmp1</i> and <i>mmp13</i> upregulation. Thus, investigating how malformed craniofacial tissues are naturally corrected in <i>X. laevis</i> tadpoles has provided valuable insights into the maintenance and manipulation of craniofacial morphology in a vertebrate system. These insights may help in the development of novel therapies for developmental craniofacial anomalies in humans.","affiliation":"Department of Biology, Allen Discovery Center, Tufts University, 200 Boston Ave., Suite 4700, Medford, MA 02155, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"The Paul G. Allen Frontiers Group","grantId":"12171","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Embryo, Nonmammalian"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Xenopus laevis","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EM","qualifierName":"embryology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"GD","qualifierName":"growth & development"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Craniofacial Abnormalities","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Thyroid Hormones","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PD","qualifierName":"pharmacology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Receptors, Thyroid Hormone","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Adaptation, Biological","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Bone Remodeling","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Expression Regulation, Developmental","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Expression Regulation, Enzymolo